Previous article】\nL51: \nL52: 【22†Next article】\nL53: \nL54: Free access\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nDLMF: §3.3 Interpolation ‣ Areas ‣ Chapter 3 Numerical Methods (https://dlmf.nist.gov/3.3)\nciteturn10view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"https://dlmf.nist.gov/3.3\",\"pattern\":\"Newton\"}); Total lines: 857\nL24: ###### Contents\nL25: \nL26:   1. 【14†§3.3(i) Lagrange Interpolation】\nL27:   2. 【15†§3.3(ii) Lagrange Interpolation with Equally-Spaced Nodes】\nL28:   3. 【16†§3.3(iii) Divided Differences】\nL29:   4. 【17†§3.3(iv) Newton’s Interpolation Formula】\nL30:   5. 【18†§3.3(v) Inverse Interpolation】\nL31:   6. 【19†§3.3(vi) Other Interpolation Methods】\nL699: ## §3.3(iv) Newton’s Interpolation Formula\nL700: \nL701: ⓘ\nL702: \nL703: Keywords:\nL704:     【22†Lagrange interpolation】, 【339†Newton’s interpolation formula】\nL705: Notes:\nL706:     See Davis (【32†1975】, pp. 39–49) and Hildebrand (【312†1974】, pp. 60–66).\nL707: Permalink:\nL708:     【340†http://dlmf.nist.gov/3.3.iv】\nL709: See also:\nL710:     Annotations for 【44†§3.3】 and 【13†Ch.3】\nL711: \nL712: This represents the Lagrange interpolation polynomial in terms of divided differences:\nL713: 3.3.38  |  | $$f(z)=\\begin{array}{l}\\left[{z}_{0}\\right]f+(z-{z}_{0})\\left[{z}_{0},{z}_{1}\\right]f+(z-{z}_{0})(z-{z}_{1})\\left[{z}_{0},{z}_{1},{z}_{2}\\right]f\\\\ \\phantom{\\rule{2em}{0ex}}+\\mathrm{\\cdots }+(z-{z}_{0})(z-{z}_{1})\\mathrm{\\cdots }(z-{z}_{n-1})\\left[{z}_{0},{z}_{1},\\mathrm{\\dots },{z}_{n}\\right]f+{R}_{n}(z).\\end{array}$$  |\nL714: ⓘ\nL715: Symbols:\nL716:     【326†$\\left[{z}_{0},{z}_{1},\\mathrm{\\dots },{z}_{n}\\right]f$: divided difference】, 【45†$f(z)$: function】, 【45†${z}_{k}$: nodes of function】 and 【77†${R}_{n}(x)$: remainder】\nL717: A&S Ref:\nL718:     25.2.26\nL719: Referenced by:\nL720:     【341†§3.3(v)】, 【342†§3.3(v)】, 【38†§3.3(iv)】\nL721: Permalink:\nL722:     【343†http://dlmf.nist.gov/3.3.E38】\nL723: Encodings:\nL724:     【344†TeX】, 【345†pMML】, 【346†png】\nL725: See also:\nL726:     Annotations for 【347†§3.3(iv)】, 【44†§3.3】 and 【13†Ch.3】\nL727: The interpolation error ${R}_{n}(z)$ is as in §【14†3.3(i)】. Newton’s formula has the advantage of allowing easy updating: incorporation of a new point ${z}_{n+1}$ requires only addition of the term with $\\left[{z}_{0},{z}_{1},\\mathrm{\\dots },{z}_{n+1}\\right]f$ to (【348†3.3.38】), plus the computation of this divided difference. Another advantage is its robustness with respect to confluence of the set of points ${z}_{0},{z}_{1},\\mathrm{\\dots },{z}_{n}$.\nL769: Symbols:\nL770:     【45†$f(z)$: function】\nL771: Permalink:\nL772:     【364†http://dlmf.nist.gov/3.3.E40】\nL773: Encodings:\nL774:     【365†TeX】, 【366†pMML】, 【367†png】\nL775: See also:\nL776:     Annotations for 【363†§3.3(v)】, 【357†§3.3(v)】, 【44†§3.3】 and 【13†Ch.3】\nL777: and with $f=0$ we find that $x=-\\mathrm{2.33823\\hspace{0.33em}2462}$, with 4 correct digits. By using this approximation to $x$ as a new point, ${x}_{3}=x$, and evaluating $\\left[{f}_{0},{f}_{1},{f}_{2},{f}_{3}\\right]x=\\mathrm{1.12388\\hspace{0.33em}6190}$, we find that $x=-\\mathrm{2.33810\\hspace{0.33em}7409}$, with 9 correct digits.\nL778: \nL779: For comparison, we use Newton’s interpolation formula (【348†3.3.38】)\nL780: 3.3.41  |  | $$f(x)=\\mathrm{0.09614\\hspace{0.33em}53780}+\\mathrm{0.69439\\hspace{0.33em}04495}(x+2.1)-\\mathrm{0.03007\\hspace{0.33em}14275}(x+2.2)(x+2.3),$$  |\nL781: ⓘ\nL782: \nL783: Symbols:\nL784:     【45†$f(z)$: function】\nL785: Permalink:\nL786:     【368†http://dlmf.nist.gov/3.3.E41】\nL787: Encodings:\nL788:     【369†TeX】, 【370†pMML】, 【371†png】\nL789: See also:\nL790:     Annotations for 【363†§3.3(v)】, 【357†§3.3(v)】, 【44†§3.3】 and 【13†Ch.3】\nL791: \nL792: with the derivative\nL793: 3.3.42  |  | $${f}^{\\prime }(x)=\\mathrm{0.55906\\hspace{0.33em}90257}-\\mathrm{0.06014\\hspace{0.33em}28550}x,$$  |\nL794: ⓘ\nL795: \nL796: Symbols:\nL797:     【45†$f(z)$: function】\nL798: Permalink:\nL799:     【372†http://dlmf.nist.gov/3.3.E42】\nL800: Encodings:\nL801:     【373†TeX】, 【374†pMML】, 【375†png】\nL802: See also:\nL803:     Annotations for 【363†§3.3(v)】, 【357†§3.3(v)】, 【44†§3.3】 and 【13†Ch.3】\nL804: and compute an approximation to ${a}_{1}$ by using Newton’s rule (§【376†3.8(ii)】) with starting value $x=-2.5$. This gives the new point ${x}_{3}=-\\mathrm{2.33934\\hspace{0.33em}0514}$.\nL805: Then by using ${x}_{3}$ in Newton’s interpolation formula, evaluating $\\left[{x}_{0},{x}_{1},{x}_{2},{x}_{3}\\right]f=-\\mathrm{0.26608\\hspace{0.33em}28233}$ and recomputing ${f}^{\\prime }(x)$, another application of Newton’s rule with starting value ${x}_{3}$ gives the approximation $x=\\mathrm{2.33810\\hspace{0.33em}7373}$, with 8 correct digits.\nL806: ## §3.3(vi) Other Interpolation Methods\nL807: \nL808: ⓘ\nL809: \nL810: Keywords:\nL811:     【377†Hermite】, 【378†Sinc function】, 【379†based on Chebyshev points】, 【380†based on Sinc functions】, 【381†bivariate】, 【382†cardinal function】, 【383†convergence properties】, 【11†interpolation】, 【384†numerical differentiation】, 【385†rational】, 【386†spline】, 【387†trigonometric】\nL812: Referenced by:\nL813:     【388†§3.4(i)】, 【389†§3.5(i)】\nL814: Permalink:\nL815:     【390†http://dlmf.nist.gov/3.3.vi】\nL816: See also:\nL817:     Annotations for 【44†§3.3】 and 【13†Ch.3】\nL818: For Hermite interpolation, trigonometric interpolation, spline interpolation, rational interpolation (by using continued fractions), interpolation based on Chebyshev points, and bivariate interpolation, see Bulirsch and Rutishauser (【391†1968】), Davis (【32†1975】, pp. 27–31), and Mason and Handscomb (【392†2003】, Chapter 6). These references also describe convergence properties of the interpolation formulas.\nL819: \nL820: For interpolation of a bounded function $f$ on $ℝ$ the cardinal function of $f$ is defined by\nL821: 3.3.43  |  | $$C(f,h)(x)=\\sum _{k=-\\mathrm{\\infty }}^{\\mathrm{\\infty }}f(kh)S(k,h)(x),$$  |\nL822: ⓘ\nL823: \nL824: Defines:\nL825:     $C(f,h)$: cardinal function of $f$ (locally)\nL826: Symbols:\nL827:     【45†$f(z)$: function】 and 【393†$S(k,h)(x)$: Sinc function】\nL828: Permalink:\nL829:     【394†http://dlmf.nist.gov/3.3.E43】\nL830: Encodings:\nL831:     【395†TeX】, 【396†pMML】, 【397†png】\nL832: See also:\nL833:     Annotations for 【398†§3.3(vi)】, 【44†§3.3】 and 【13†Ch.3】\nL834: \nL835: where\nL836: \nL837: 3.3.44  |  | $$S(k,h)(x)=\\frac{\\mathrm{sin}\\left(\\pi (x-kh)/h\\right)}{\\pi (x-kh)/h},$$  |\nL838: ⓘ\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nDLMF: §3.3 Interpolation ‣ Areas ‣ Chapter 3 Numerical Methods (https://dlmf.nist.gov/3.3)\nciteturn10view2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"https://dlmf.nist.gov/3.3\",\"pattern\":\"Forward\"}); Total lines: 857\nNo matching text found for \"Forward\"--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nstatesync-gkr/LICENSE at main · Oraclizer/statesync-gkr · GitHub (https://github.com/Oraclizer/statesync-gkr/blob/main/LICENSE)\nciteturn10view3 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 5 days ago; Content type: text/html; Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"https://github.com/Oraclizer/statesync-gkr/blob/main/LICENSE\",\"pattern\":\"Business Source License 1.1\"}); Total lines: 565\nL169: ## File metadata and controls\nL170: \nL171:   * Code\nL172: \nL173:   * Blame\nL174: \nL175: 112 lines (82 loc) · 5.09 KB\nL176: \nL177: 【69†Raw】\nL178: \nL179: Copy raw file\nL180: \nL181: Download raw file\nL182: \nL183: Open symbols panel\nL184: \nL185: Edit and raw actions\nL186: \nL187: 1\nL188: \nL189: 2\nL190: \nL191: 3\nL192: \nL193: 4\nL194: \nL195: 5\nL196: \nL197: 6\nL198: \nL199: 7\nL200: \nL201: 8\nL362: 89\nL363: \nL364: 90\nL365: \nL366: 91\nL367: \nL368: 92\nL369: \nL370: 93\nL371: \nL372: 94\nL373: \nL374: 95\nL375: \nL376: 96\nL377: \nL378: 97\nL379: \nL380: 98\nL381: \nL382: 99\nL383: \nL384: 10